    "http://purl.org/dc/terms/abstract": [
      {
        "@value": "The MIBB Registry (Minimally Invasive Breast Biopsy Registry) is a nationwide clinical quality registry established to document and monitor minimally invasive breast biopsy procedures in Switzerland. The registry was created following a requirement by the Swiss Federal Office of Public Health (FOPH) that vacuum-assisted breast biopsies be systematically recorded as a condition for inclusion in the mandatory health insurance reimbursement catalogue. Since its inception, the registry has been operated in collaboration with the MIBB Working Group of the Swiss Society of Senology and supported by the Adjumed electronic documentation platform.  The primary objective of the project is to ensure high-quality, safe, and standardized breast biopsy procedures across Switzerland. Additional goals include supporting physician certification and training, facilitating benchmarking among participating institutions, and providing reliable real-world data for clinical evaluation and research.  The registry employs a prospective observational methodology, collecting standardized data on patient characteristics, indications for biopsy, procedural details, histopathological findings, and clinical outcomes. Data are entered by participating centers into a centralized database, enabling continuous quality monitoring, performance assessment, and comparative analyses across institutions.  Expected outcomes include improved diagnostic accuracy, enhanced adherence to clinical guidelines, identification of best practices, and optimization of patient care pathways. Furthermore, the registry provides a unique research infrastructure and has already contributed to scientific studies and international consensus recommendations, particularly regarding breast lesions of uncertain malignant potential (B3 lesions).  As one of the largest databases for vacuum-assisted breast biopsies in Europe, the MIBB Registry represents an important tool for quality assurance, clinical research, and the continuous advancement of evidence-based breast care in Switzerland."
      }
    ],
